    "interpretation_limit": "7 ranking questions (3 coauthor_breadth, 1 max_team_size with a ranked key, 3 bridge) x 2 runs, of which 6 are scorable. For q03 (max_team_size) all 100 key entries tie at the same value (15) and the key keeps the first 100 by id order, so a tied researcher outside those 100 scores 0 although it ties. q03 is listed per question and excluded from the main aggregates; a second aggregate counts it at its measured 0 (a lower bound). The tie premise is tested in tie_check from the values the answer states for the three retrieved researchers outside the key (those values were checked against the export by the Q12 scorer; this script does not read the export): one states 15 (a tie cut off by the key's 100-entry limit), one states 14 (below the key's value, so genuinely not a top-ranked researcher for this measure), and for one no value is stated (the answer lists 2 of the 3 retrieved). So the 0 is only partly an artifact of the tie truncation. The same truncation can affect a few tied items at the foot of the other keys. The two runs retrieved identical lists (observed), so they are not independent samples and no confidence interval is given (n = 7 questions would make one meaningless). The key's top-100 is the corpus-wide ranking while the runtime ranks inside its bounded artifact, so P@K against the key's top-100 is a looser test than against its top-K. Graded gain = the key's metric value, a derived grade, not a human relevance judgement. K is the runtime's own context cut (3 or 4 researcher items), so recall@K against the 100-item key is not reported (it would be at most K/100 by construction).",
